visualization_scene = ExtResource("2_mr84h")
concept_text = "Merge Sort is a divide-and-conquer algorithm that ALWAYS runs in O(n log n) — even in the worst case. Unlike Quick Sort, it never degrades to O(n²).

It works in two clear phases:

PHASE 1 — SPLIT: keep cutting the array in half until you have many tiny arrays of size 1. (A single item is always \"sorted\".)

PHASE 2 — MERGE: merge the tiny arrays back together in sorted order. The merge step compares the FRONTS of two sorted halves and takes the smaller one each time.

Imagine sorting [38, 27, 43, 3]:

Phase 1 — Split:
[38, 27, 43, 3]
    ↓ split
[38, 27]    [43, 3]
    ↓ split        ↓ split
[38]  [27]    [43]  [3]

Now we have four arrays of size 1. Each is trivially sorted.

Phase 2 — Merge:
Merge [38] and [27]: compare 38 and 27, take 27 first, then 38 → [27, 38]
Merge [43] and [3]:  compare 43 and 3, take 3 first, then 43 → [3, 43]
Merge [27, 38] and [3, 43]:
  Compare 27 and 3 → take 3
  Compare 27 and 43 → take 27
  Compare 38 and 43 → take 38
  Then 43 → [3, 27, 38, 43]

Sorted!

Performance:
- ALWAYS O(n log n) — best, average, and worst case
- Space: O(n) — needs an extra array to merge into (the main downside)
- STABLE: equal elements keep their original order
- Easy to understand once you \"see\" the recursion
- Used in: Python's built-in sort (Timsort is a hybrid of merge sort and insertion sort), external sorting of huge files, sorting linked lists"

real_world_example = "Imagine you have a huge stack of unsorted papers. You want to sort them by date.

Step 1 — Split. You divide the stack in half. Each half is still unsorted, but smaller. You keep dividing until each stack is just ONE paper. (One paper is trivially sorted — you know its date.)

Step 2 — Merge. Now you start combining stacks back together, but you MERGE them in order. You have two sorted stacks of one paper each. You compare the TOP of each stack and take the EARLIER one. Repeat until one stack is empty. Then put the rest of the other stack on top.

You keep merging until all papers are in one big stack, sorted by date.

Another example: combining two sorted lists of names. Say one list is [Alice, Bob, Charlie] and the other is [Anna, Brian, Dave]. To merge them sorted:
- Compare A (Alice) and A (Anna) — Anna comes first alphabetically
- Compare A (Alice) and B (Brian) — Alice comes first
- Compare B (Bob) and B (Brian) — Brian
- Compare B (Bob) and D (Dave) — Bob
- Then Charlie, then Dave

Result: [Anna, Alice, Brian, Bob, Charlie, Dave]

That's the MERGE step of merge sort. The key insight: when you merge two SORTED lists, you only need to compare the FRONTS — you never have to look inside either list.

A third example: how Python's built-in sort() works. It's called Timsort, and it's a hybrid of merge sort and insertion sort. For real-world data (which is often already partly sorted), Timsort is extremely fast — usually faster than Quick Sort in practice.

Why use merge sort? Because it's RELIABLE — it always runs in O(n log n), no matter what the input looks like. The downside is it needs O(n) extra memory."

guided_example = "Let's trace Merge Sort on [38, 27, 43, 3], level by level.

PHASE 1 — SPLIT (top-down)

Start:  [38, 27, 43, 3]      (4 items)
         ↓ split into halves
Level 1: [38, 27]  [43, 3]   (2 + 2 items)
         ↓          ↓
Level 2: [38] [27] [43] [3]   (1 + 1 + 1 + 1 items)

Now we have 4 arrays of size 1. Each is trivially sorted.

PHASE 2 — MERGE (bottom-up)

Merge [38] and [27]:
- Compare 38 and 27. 27 is smaller, take it.
- 38 is left, take it.
- Result: [27, 38]

Merge [43] and [3]:
- Compare 43 and 3. 3 is smaller, take it.
- 43 is left, take it.
- Result: [3, 43]

Merge [27, 38] and [3, 43]:
- Compare 27 and 3. 3 is smaller, take it. → [3]
- Compare 27 and 43. 27 is smaller, take it. → [3, 27]
- Compare 38 and 43. 38 is smaller, take it. → [3, 27, 38]
- 43 is left, take it. → [3, 27, 38, 43]

Final result: [3, 27, 38, 43]. SORTED!

The KEY TRICK is the merge step. To merge two SORTED arrays:
1. Compare the front of each.
2. Take the smaller one.
3. Repeat until one array is empty.
4. Append the rest of the other array.

This is always O(n) per merge, where n is the total size of the two arrays being merged. With log n levels, total work is O(n log n)."

recap_text = "- Merge Sort uses divide-and-conquer: split, recurse, merge
- PHASE 1: split the array in half until you have arrays of size 1
- PHASE 2: merge the small arrays back together in sorted order
- The merge step: compare fronts of two sorted halves, take the smaller
- ALWAYS O(n log n) — best, average, AND worst case
- Space: O(n) — needs an extra array (main downside)
- STABLE: equal elements keep their original order
- Great for: linked lists (no extra memory needed), external sorting of huge files
- Python's built-in sort is a hybrid of merge sort and insertion sort (Timsort)
- Recursive — each level splits the array in half
- The merge step is the heart of the algorithm"

